solnix: tzmon -- act on ThinkPad thermal alarms reliably: ThinkPad HKEY init, debounced _HOT/_CRT, and a clean power-off.

WHAT THE X1 EXTREME GEN5 (21DE001EUS) SHOWED
- The stock tzmon halted the machine at "Thermal zone (\_TZ_.THM0) is critically hot (128 C); initiating rapid
  shutdown" -> "Press any key to reboot." (photographed 2026-10-17 and 2026-10-18, 40-60 s after login).
- An earlier version of this patch treated _TMP == _CRT as a firmware sentinel and skipped the shutdown. THAT WAS
  WRONG. With it the machine ran on, then lost power silently, about 20 minutes after boot. That was the EC's own
  hardware cutoff (2026-10-20).

WHAT THE FIRMWARE ACTUALLY DOES (ThinkPad X1 Extreme / P1 and X1 Carbon DSDTs)
- \_TZ.THM0._TMP reads EC TMP0. An unreadable sensor (0x80) becomes 0x30, which is 48 C. _C2K clamps
  out-of-range values to 48 C as well.
- _TMP returns exactly TCRT (== _CRT == 128 C) only when the EC raises a temperature level:
  - always when TSL3 is non-zero;
  - when TSL2 is non-zero AND HKEY.DHKC is 0, that is, no OS driver has enabled the hotkey interface.
- So _TMP == _CRT is the EC's critical alarm, not a bad read. It must be acted on.
- Under Linux (thinkpad_acpi sets DHKC=1 at load) the same machine idles at 44-50 C, fan on auto.

THE CHANGE
1. ThinkPad init, the platform shim. tzmon_attach finds HKEY (_HID LEN0268/LEN0068/IBM0068), installs a notify
   handler that drains MHKP and logs the thermal-alarm events (0x6011/0x6012/0x6021/0x6022) with the EC's TSL0-3
   bitmaps, then calls MHKC(1), as Linux thinkpad_acpi does at load. After that the EC reports its TSL2 level as event
   0x6022 instead of forcing _TMP to _CRT. TSL3, the EC's own critical level, still reads as _CRT.
2. Debounce, FreeBSD's model (sys/dev/acpica/acpi_thermal.c TZ_VALIDCHECKS):
   - _HOT and _CRT act only after TZ_VALIDCHECKS (3) consecutive over-limit polls;
   - while a zone is over a limit it is re-polled every TZ_CONFIRM_PERIOD (2) seconds, so confirming takes about
     4 s, not 3 x 30 s;
   - one bogus EC read no longer takes the machine down, and a sustained alarm still does.
3. Both limits power off through init (SIGPWR -> inittab powerfail -> shutdown -i5), so filesystems sync. Before this
   patch, _CRT halted at "Press any key to reboot." and left a hot machine running.
4. Every over-limit poll prints one console line: the reading, _HOT/_CRT, the count, the EC's sensor temperatures
   TMP0-7 (0 and +/-128 = no sensor, as FreeBSD acpi_ibm.c reads them) and TSL0-3. The console then names the sensor
   behind the alarm.

The model-independent part is (2)-(3). (1) binds on the ThinkPad HKEY _HID and is a no-op elsewhere.
--- a/usr/src/uts/i86pc/io/tzmon/tzmon.h
+++ b/usr/src/uts/i86pc/io/tzmon/tzmon.h
@@ -35,6 +35,10 @@
 #define	TZ_DEFAULT_PERIOD	30
 
 #define	TZ_NUM_LEVELS		10
+/* consecutive over-limit polls before tzmon acts (FreeBSD TZ_VALIDCHECKS) */
+#define	TZ_VALIDCHECKS		3
+/* poll period while a zone is over a limit, so confirming takes seconds */
+#define	TZ_CONFIRM_PERIOD	2
 
 typedef struct thermal_zone {
 	struct thermal_zone	*next;
@@ -56,6 +60,14 @@
 
 	int			polling_period;
 	int			current_level;
+
+	/*
+	 * solnix: consecutive polls at or above _HOT/_CRT. tzmon acts only once
+	 * this reaches TZ_VALIDCHECKS, so a single bad EC read does not power
+	 * the machine off, while a sustained alarm still does (FreeBSD
+	 * acpi_thermal.c tz_validchecks).
+	 */
+	int			validchecks;
 } thermal_zone_t;
 
 
